.TH EUCLID-CLI-ESM-PURGE-BUCKET 1 "August 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-esm-purge-bucket \- remove all objects from a bucket
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli esm purge-bucket
.BR \-\-bucket | \-b
.I ERN
[\fB\-\-prefix\fR|\fB\-p\fR \fIPREFIX\fR]
[\fB\-\-async\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-notify\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Removes objects from a bucket identified by its Euclid resource name
(ERN), leaving the bucket itself in place; see
.BR euclid-cli-esm-delete-bucket (1)
to remove the bucket too. This cannot be undone.
.PP
By default every object in the bucket is removed. If
.B \-\-prefix
is given, only objects whose key starts with it are removed, so the
bucket may still hold non-matching objects afterward.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bucket " " \fIERN\fR
Euclid resource name of the bucket to purge. Required.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-prefix " " \fIPREFIX\fR
Only remove objects whose key starts with this prefix. Optional; if
omitted, every object in the bucket is removed.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-notify
Remove the objects without announcing any of them.
.PP
Every removed object is normally published as one
.I esm.object.deleted
event to each of the bucket's subscribers. That is one event per object
and not one per purge, deliberately: a subscriber keeping its own index
of what the bucket holds has to be told which keys went, and "the bucket
was purged" does not say.
.PP
It is also not free at either end. A purge of 1.3 million objects fed a
listener's queue at roughly 1,600 messages a minute for hours, and the
backlog held that application's pool at its ceiling for the duration \-
so the cheap half of the work was the deleting and the expensive half
was telling everyone about it.
.PP
Use this when clearing test data, or any bucket whose subscribers do not
care what was in it. Do not use it where a subscriber keeps a record of
the bucket's contents: that record goes stale silently and stays stale.
There is no later event that reconciles it, and
.BR euclid-cli-esm-touch-object (1)
re\-announces only what is still in the bucket, never what was removed.
.PP
The choice is recorded on the job, so an
.B \-\-async
purge picked up by another instance, or after a restart, stays silent.
.TP
.B \-\-async
Answer at once and remove the objects in the background, for a bucket
large enough that emptying it takes minutes.
.PP
Without it the whole purge happens inside the request: far longer than
the gateway's backend timeout on a large bucket, so the call fails with
a timeout while the purge carries on unseen behind the abandoned
request. With it the answer is
.I "HTTP 202"
carrying the object count at the time of asking, and a thread inside ESM
does the work.
.PP
That thread works a page at a time \-
.I euclid.modules.esm.purge\-page\-size
objects, a thousand unless the installation says otherwise \- and
repeats until the bucket has nothing left under the prefix. Two things
follow. It does not build the whole object list in memory first, which
on a large enough bucket was the allocation that ended the process and
the purge with it. And it finishes when the bucket is empty rather than
when a list taken at the start is exhausted, so a bucket still being
written to \- by a transfer server delivering into it, say \- is
emptied rather than reported done with objects still in it.
.PP
A run that is interrupted has removed whole pages, so asking again takes
the rest. If a page comes back and nothing in it can be removed, the
thread stops and says so rather than asking for the same page for ever;
the log line names the bucket and the prefix.
.SH IT SURVIVES THE INSTANCE THAT ACCEPTED IT
The request is written down before the work starts, and the record is
removed only when the work is done. The
.I "HTTP 202"
carries its
.I jobId
\- that record's name.
.PP
This matters because a thread is not something the rest of euclid can
see. Having answered, the instance looks idle to the autoscaler, which
stops idle instances; a crash, an
.B "emm restart-module"
or a manager shutdown do the same. Before the job record, every one of
those ended the removal silently, with nothing left to say it had been
asked for.
.PP
Now any ESM instance picks up a removal whose worker has stopped. Each
instance sweeps every
.I euclid.modules.esm.purge\-sweep\-period
seconds for a job whose claim has not been refreshed within
.IR euclid.modules.esm.purge\-claim\-stale\-seconds ,
and carries it on from the next page. A worker refreshes its claim after
every page, so "stale" means no page has finished recently rather than
"this has been running a while" \- a large bucket is legitimately slow
and is not taken from a worker that is getting on with it.
.PP
The autoscaler is also told: an instance reports how much background
work it is doing, and one reporting any is passed over when an idle
instance is chosen. That keeps a removal from being interrupted in the
first place; the job record is what makes it survive when something
interrupts it anyway.
.PP
The synchronous form still takes the whole list at once and removes what
existed when it started. That is what makes it answerable inside a
request, and why a bucket too big for that is what
.B \-\-async
is for.
.PP
The bucket's counters are adjusted when the removal finishes, by the
database rather than by the thread \- it says how far to move them, not
what they should be. So anything uploaded while the purge ran is still
counted, and several purges of one bucket at once each land instead of
the last one to finish overwriting the others. Watch the progress with
.BR euclid-cli-esm-get-bucket-size (1).
.PP
Running more than one purge on the same bucket at once is therefore safe
for the counters, and still wasteful: both threads walk the same objects
and the second finds most of them already gone. One purge with the
broadest
.B \-\-prefix
that covers what you want does the same work once.
.PP
The counters are in any case only ever a running total. The monitoring
module recomputes them from what each bucket actually holds every
.I euclid.modules.emo.bucket\-count\-period
seconds - sixty in the shipped configuration - so a figure that drifted
for any reason corrects itself within that.
.PP
The bucket may be given by
.B name
instead of by ERN, in which case it is resolved in your session's account
and namespace; see
.B NAMES AND ERNS
in
.BR euclid-cli-esm (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The matching objects were deleted; a summary
.RI ( "{ern, count}" )
is printed as JSON, where
.I count
is the number of objects removed.
.TP
.B 1
Missing/invalid arguments, the server could not be reached, or no
bucket with that ERN exists.
.SH EXAMPLES
Remove every object from a bucket:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 esm purge-bucket \e
    --bucket ern:esm:eu-central-1:000000000000:bucket:reports
.fi
.RE
.PP
Remove only objects under a given key prefix:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 esm purge-bucket \e
    --bucket ern:esm:eu-central-1:000000000000:bucket:reports \e
    --prefix 2025/
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-esm (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-delete-bucket (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-delete-object (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-list-objects (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-get-object-count (1)
